/* =========================
   19. EXISTS / NOT EXISTS
   =========================
   DEF: Check whether a subquery returns any rows
   WHEN: Check if related records exist
   WHERE: Filtering based on another table


   EXISTS
   DEF: Returns TRUE if the subquery returns at least one row

   INPUT: SELECT * FROM student s
          WHERE EXISTS (
              SELECT 1
              FROM orders o
              WHERE o.student_id = s.id
          );

   OUTPUT: Students who have at least one order
   REAL: Find users who have placed an order


   NOT EXISTS
   DEF: Returns TRUE if the subquery returns no rows

   INPUT: SELECT * FROM student s
          WHERE NOT EXISTS (
              SELECT 1
              FROM orders o
              WHERE o.student_id = s.id
          );

   OUTPUT: Students who have no orders
   REAL: Find users who have never placed an order


   IMPORTANT:

   EXISTS     → Related record EXISTS
   NOT EXISTS → Related record DOES NOT EXIST


   SIMPLE EXAMPLE:

   student → 1, 2, 3
   orders  → student_id 1, 3

   EXISTS     → 1, 3
   NOT EXISTS → 2
*/
/* =========================
   20. UNION / UNION ALL
   =========================
   DEF: Combine results of multiple SELECT queries
   WHEN: Combine similar data from multiple queries/tables
   WHERE: Reports, search results, data aggregation


   UNION
   DEF: Combines results and removes duplicate rows

   INPUT: SELECT name FROM student_2025
          UNION
          SELECT name FROM student_2026;

   OUTPUT: Combined unique names
   REAL: Combine students from two years without duplicates


   UNION ALL
   DEF: Combines results and keeps duplicate rows

   INPUT: SELECT name FROM student_2025
          UNION ALL
          SELECT name FROM student_2026;

   OUTPUT: Combined names including duplicates
   REAL: Combine all records without removing duplicates


   IMPORTANT:

   UNION     → Duplicate rows REMOVE
   UNION ALL → Duplicate rows KEEP


   RULES:
   1. Both SELECT queries must have the same number of columns
   2. Corresponding columns should have compatible data types
   3. Column names come from the first SELECT


   SIMPLE EXAMPLE:

   Table 1 → Anup, Rahul
   Table 2 → Rahul, Amit

   UNION     → Anup, Rahul, Amit
   UNION ALL → Anup, Rahul, Rahul, Amit
*/
/* =========================
