/* =========================
   27. MYSQL INDEX
   =========================
   DEF: Index is used to make data searching faster
   WHEN: When a table has many rows and we frequently search/filter data
   WHERE: WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY, UNIQUE columns


   CREATE INDEX
   DEF: Creates an index on a column

   INPUT: CREATE INDEX idx_name
          ON student(name);

   OUTPUT: Index created on name
   REAL: Faster searching by name


   MULTIPLE COLUMN INDEX
   DEF: Index on more than one column

   INPUT: CREATE INDEX idx_student
          ON student(name, age);

   OUTPUT: Index created on name and age
   REAL: Faster queries using name + age


   UNIQUE INDEX
   DEF: Prevents duplicate values and improves searching

   INPUT: CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_email
          ON student(email);

   OUTPUT: Duplicate email values are not allowed
   REAL: Keep email values unique


   SHOW INDEX
   DEF: Shows indexes of a table

   INPUT: SHOW INDEX FROM student;

   OUTPUT: Displays indexes of student table
   REAL: Check which indexes exist


   DROP INDEX
   DEF: Removes an index

   INPUT: DROP INDEX idx_name
          ON student;

   OUTPUT: Index removed
   REAL: Remove an unnecessary index


   PRIMARY KEY INDEX
   DEF: PRIMARY KEY automatically creates an index

   INPUT: CREATE TABLE student(
          id INT PRIMARY KEY,
          name VARCHAR(50)
          );

   OUTPUT: id is automatically indexed
   REAL: Fast lookup using student ID


   IMPORTANT:

   INDEX → Makes searching faster

   CREATE INDEX → Create index
   UNIQUE INDEX → No duplicate values
   SHOW INDEX   → See indexes
   DROP INDEX   → Remove index

   ⚠️ Index improves SELECT/search performance,
      but too many indexes can slow INSERT, UPDATE
      and DELETE operations because indexes also need
      to be updated.
*/





































